WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.377
(upbeat instrumental music)

00:00:02.377 --> 00:00:05.852
- If you go into the water and
you see a great white shark,

00:00:05.852 --> 00:00:06.795
what's your reaction?

00:00:06.795 --> 00:00:08.157
You're not gonna think twice.

00:00:08.157 --> 00:00:12.038
Pfft, you're haulin'
ass to get outta there.

00:00:12.038 --> 00:00:15.180
Falcons are the great
white shark of the sky,

00:00:15.180 --> 00:00:17.847
and birds are terrified of them.

00:00:21.146 --> 00:00:22.607
My name is Mark Adam.

00:00:22.607 --> 00:00:26.180
I am the president of Falcon
Environmental Services.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:30.227
Our company uses the world's
oldest sport, falconry,

00:00:30.227 --> 00:00:32.560
to protect planes in flight.

00:00:35.708 --> 00:00:39.931
Birds can be really
dangerous to airplanes.

00:00:39.931 --> 00:00:43.584
If a gull strikes an engine,
it can potentially stop it,

00:00:43.584 --> 00:00:48.444
in which case the aircraft
can come out of the sky.

00:00:48.444 --> 00:00:49.978
There are multiple methods

00:00:49.978 --> 00:00:52.833
that airports can use to control birds.

00:00:52.833 --> 00:00:53.891
Sirens.

00:00:53.891 --> 00:00:57.456
Another very common one is pyrotechnics.

00:00:57.456 --> 00:00:59.673
Birds are not dumb.

00:00:59.673 --> 00:01:01.318
They get used to that stuff.

00:01:01.318 --> 00:01:03.737
But one thing that they
can never get used to,

00:01:03.737 --> 00:01:06.737
is the instinctive fear of a falcon.

00:01:09.816 --> 00:01:11.583
Here we raise falcons,

00:01:11.583 --> 00:01:12.703
and, when they hatch out,

00:01:12.703 --> 00:01:15.019
we rear them for three or four days,

00:01:15.019 --> 00:01:17.852
and put 'em back with the parents.

00:01:18.707 --> 00:01:21.340
The skills that we use
to train our raptors

00:01:21.340 --> 00:01:25.507
date back to 3000 B.C., when
falconry was first introduced,

00:01:27.764 --> 00:01:31.931
utilizing hoods, jesses,
and conditioning methods.

00:01:33.650 --> 00:01:37.030
The birds are trained
for about 30 to 60 days,

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:41.113
where then they go out to
our airport operations.

00:01:43.095 --> 00:01:45.995
On a given day, they are
requested numerous times

00:01:45.995 --> 00:01:49.155
by the control tower to
intervene at specific spots

00:01:49.155 --> 00:01:53.259
on the airfield, at which
time, the falcon is deployed,

00:01:53.259 --> 00:01:55.590
and it is allowed to
fly around the airport

00:01:55.590 --> 00:01:58.798
until the birds have been dispersed.

00:01:58.798 --> 00:02:01.778
When the falconer wants
the falcon to come back,

00:02:01.778 --> 00:02:04.227
all he has to do is take out the lure,

00:02:04.227 --> 00:02:07.368
which has a piece of food on
it, swing it around his head,

00:02:07.368 --> 00:02:11.368
and the falcon will come
in, in order to be fed.

00:02:13.689 --> 00:02:15.211
I enjoy birdwatching,

00:02:15.211 --> 00:02:17.766
but I find falconry almost like a chance

00:02:17.766 --> 00:02:20.916
to see something that happens
every day in the wild,

00:02:20.916 --> 00:02:24.999
but I get a chance to see
it up close every day.

